Formatter: format any screen, and terminal state Muster reads

Muster replays a pane's terminal into a fresh one when a surface attaches
(docs/mip/0003-own-daemon.md, section 5). The pinned C formatter reaches
only the active screen, and always emits content with the terminal's
extras. So it cannot reproduce the primary screen while a program holds
the alternate one, and cannot emit tabstops, the scrolling region or the
pen apart from content. formatter.zig's own comment on TerminalFormatter
names the way out: a no-content terminal formatter followed by screen
formatters. This exposes both to C:

- ghostty_formatter_terminal_state_new: the terminal formatter with
  content none. Same options; the selection must be NULL.
- ghostty_formatter_screen_new: a screen formatter for the primary or
  alternate screen, looked up on every format call. It can omit content,
  can leave out history and emit only the active area, and can emit the
  blank rows below the last row with text, which the formatter always
  drops, so that its output spans every row. Leaving out history is how
  a surface that fell behind is caught up with the screen alone.
- Three formatter.Options, each default false, which only
  ghostty_formatter_screen_new turns on:
  - unstyled_blanks. The formatter writes a run of blank cells before
    the next text in whatever style the previous text left open, so a
    background or underline bleeds across the gap; with the option it
    closes the style first.
  - styled_blank_rows. The formatter drops a row with no text as blank,
    and with it a background the row was erased with; with the option a
    painted row is written, as the content loop already writes such
    cells.
  - vt_hyperlinks. VT output writes no hyperlinks, so a link on text
    already written is lost; with the option VT output writes OSC 8
    where HTML output writes a link, and closes it across cells nobody
    wrote, which belong to no link.

It also adds, in src/terminal/c/muster.zig and its header
include/ghostty/vt/muster.h, terminal state the C API does not reach:

- ghostty_terminal_screen_cursor_style: either screen's cursor style.
  DECSCUSR sets the active screen's only, and the render state reads
  only the active one.
- ghostty_terminal_mouse_tracking: the tracking mode and format in
  effect, which the mode bits cannot say, since setting a tracking mode
  replaces the last without clearing its bit.
- ghostty_terminal_mouse_shift_capture: what XTSHIFTESCAPE set.
- ghostty_terminal_clear_screen: the terminal half of Ghostty's own
  clear_screen binding (Termio.clearScreen), for a daemon that performs
  the binding on a terminal with no Termio.
- ghostty_terminal_kitty_image_loading: whether a chunked kitty image is
  still arriving, so a caller forgetting images can wait for its end.
- ghostty_terminal_kitty_image_loading_bytes and
  ghostty_terminal_kitty_image_drop_loading: how much of that image has
  arrived, and dropping it. A program cut off between two chunks leaves
  its image arriving until a full reset, so a caller that sees it stop
  growing can let it go.

Additive only: new functions, one struct, options that default to the
existing output, no existing behavior changed.
Muster carries this for good rather than sending it upstream, so it is
kept small enough to rebase on a re-pin. The Zig tests at the end of
src/terminal/c/formatter.zig and in src/terminal/c/muster.zig cover it:
zig build test-lib-vt.

